                    briefing_text_r2:       `The core round. Redesign Liwanag’s model so that <strong>securing supply</strong> and <strong>lifting farmers</strong> become the <em>same</em> activity, not competing objectives. Scaling the foundation buys press, not advantage; squeezing procurement protects this quarter and loses the supply base and the ESG customer. A <strong>farmer-equity shared-value</strong> model or a <strong>digital farmer-services platform</strong> can secure supply <em>because</em> it lifts incomes — but only if impact is embedded structurally and the advantage rests on something a competitor cannot copy in a quarter: the relationships, the price floors, the data, the platform.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Allocate across the dual constraint`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `Allocate the <strong>₱1.2 billion</strong> three-year envelope so the plan clears the <strong>18% hurdle</strong> <em>and</em> moves the inclusive-growth index — not two separate budgets. Herrera’s point is that I4I makes impact and return the <em>same</em> activity; split them and you have relabeled charity. Then institutionalize it: a real <strong>P&amp;L owner</strong> in the core business (not a standalone sustainability office that gets cut at the first margin squeeze), the three enablers — strategic alignment, responsible purpose, institutional drive — and a single dual-constraint metric.`,
