                    coach_r3_split:         `<strong>₱800M “business” and ₱400M “impact” are two budgets — and that’s the error.</strong> Herrera’s whole point is that I4I makes them the <em>same</em> activity. If you can’t draw the line connecting the impact and the advantage, you haven’t institutionalized social innovation — you’ve relabeled charity. Show me the single mechanism that does both.`,
                    coach_r3_relief:        `<strong>₱700M into relief and scholarships is the foundation with a bigger cheque.</strong> It moves the goodwill needle and nothing else — the supply is still short and the return is below the hurdle. The envelope was meant to build advantage, not scale philanthropy.`,
                    coach_r3_thin:          `<strong>Spreading ₱1.2B thinly funds everything and institutionalizes nothing.</strong> No single mechanism gets enough capital to clear the hurdle or build a moat. Concentrate on the one activity that does both.`,
                    coach_r3_office:        `<strong>A sustainability office with no revenue accountability is the foundation in new clothes.</strong> Herrera names “institutional drive” as an enabler — the first time margins tighten, an initiative with no P&L authority gets cut. Who in the <em>core business</em> owns this P&L?`,
                    coach_r3_foundation:    `<strong>Leaving it in the foundation guarantees it stays charity.</strong> Governed by the giving committee, it will never have the operating authority to secure supply or the accountability to clear a hurdle. Institutionalizing means a P&L owner in the business.`,
                    coach_r3_purposeonly:   `<strong>A signed mission statement is necessary but nowhere near sufficient.</strong> Responsible purpose without strategic alignment and institutional drive is intent without structure — it won’t survive the first budget review. Build all three enablers.`,
