                    coach_r3_goodwillmetric: `<strong>Measuring media impressions measures charity.</strong> Goodwill is a philanthropy metric — it tells you nothing about supply secured or return earned. The scorecard metric has to bind impact and advantage together, or you’re back to the foundation.`,
                    coach_r3_undisciplined: `<strong>Loose on the dual constraint, you’ll inspire the board and miss the hurdle.</strong> A social-innovation strategy that can’t clear the cost of capital is philanthropy with a strategy deck — and it’s defunded the moment it competes for capital. The 18% discipline is what makes it genuinely strategic.`,
                    coach_r4_mechanism:     `<strong>Leading with the mechanism is the right defense.</strong> Institutionalized social innovation that secures supply <em>and</em> lifts farmers, with a moat a rival can’t copy in a quarter — that’s the case the board funds and the ESG customer credits. You’re defending strategy, not goodwill.`,
                    coach_r4_goodwill:      `<strong>Leading with the scholarships invites the killer probe.</strong> “Genuinely good — but where is the competitive advantage?” Open on goodwill and the board hears philanthropy, and reaches for the spot-market option.`,
                    coach_r4_cheap:         `<strong>Leading with cost concedes the ESG argument.</strong> The customer worth 15% of your revenue didn’t ask for the lowest price — it asked for audited inclusive sourcing. A cost story is the extractive story it’s threatening to de-list you over.`,
                    coach_r4_hold_strong:   `<strong>The model held under fire.</strong> Because the advantage sits in the relationships, the floors, the data and the platform, the shock didn’t break it — you absorbed it without reverting to charity or extraction. That resilience <em>is</em> the proof it’s strategy.`,
                    coach_r4_hold_weak:     `<strong>You held the line, but the model wasn’t resilient enough to absorb the shock.</strong> Without a real moat or secured supply behind it, “stay the course” is a slogan. The earlier rounds, not this defense, decide whether the strategy survives.`,
                    coach_r4_resp_philanthropy: `<strong>Reaching for donations under pressure is reverting to charity.</strong> Extra relief makes the headline, not the supply — and it confirms the board’s fear that this was philanthropy all along. The shock was the test of whether your strategy was real.`,
                    coach_r4_resp_extraction: `<strong>Cutting prices and running to the spot market is reverting to extraction.</strong> You absorb the cost this quarter and accelerate exactly the attrition and ESG risk the whole strategy was meant to fix. Under pressure, the model collapsed back into the problem.`,
                    coach_r4_proof_donated: `<strong>“Pesos donated” proves charity, not strategy.</strong> It’s the metric the foundation already reported. Prove strategy with secured-volume-per-income-lift, the blended return against the hurdle, and how long a rival would need to copy you.`,
                    coach_r4_proof_awareness: `<strong>Awareness and report length aren’t strategic proof.</strong> They measure visibility, not advantage. The single metric has to tie the impact to the supply and the return — anything else is the philanthropy scorecard.`,

