                    insight_institutionalized: `Institutionalized social innovation: you made securing supply and lifting farmers a single mechanism, embedded it in strategy and structure with a real P&L owner, and built a moat — relationships, price floors, platform and data — a competitor can’t copy in a quarter. The plan cleared the 18% hurdle, moved the inclusive-growth index, secured the coffee, and the ESG customer kept Liwanag listed. That is advantage, not charity.`,
                    insight_philanthropy:   `Philanthropy in disguise: you scaled the foundation’s goodwill rather than redesigning the business. The press was warm, but the coffee stayed short, the competitor kept signing your farmers, and the audited inclusive-sourcing evidence never appeared. Herrera’s verdict held — disconnected philanthropy creates goodwill, never competitive advantage — and the board reverts to the spot market.`,
                    insight_extraction:     `Extractive sourcing: you protected margin by squeezing farmers and leaning on the spot market. Costs fell for a quarter, but farmer income dropped below a living income, attrition accelerated, the supply base aged out faster, and the ESG customer de-listed you. You optimized the quarter and lost the security the strategy was about.`,
                    insight_relabeled:      `Relabeled charity: some pieces of the redesign landed, but impact and return never fused into a single mechanism with a defensible moat. Without that integration the board can’t tell strategy from a philanthropy programme with a strategy deck attached — and won’t defend it when capital gets tight.`,
                    insight_hurdle:         `The blended return never cleared the 18% hurdle. A social-innovation strategy that can’t clear the firm’s cost of capital is defunded the moment it competes for capital — the financial discipline is what forces the innovation to be genuinely strategic.`,
                    insight_impact_low:     `The inclusive-growth index stayed below the threshold: farmer income, resilience and retention didn’t move enough. Without the impact lift, “shared value” is just sourcing — and the ESG customer’s condition is unmet.`,
                    insight_supply_low:     `Coffee supply never got secured: the redesign didn’t lock in the volume the deal depended on, leaving Liwanag exposed to the spot market and the competitor’s farmer-contracting.`,
                    insight_moat_low:       `The advantage was never defensible: nothing in the model — relationships, floors, platform or data — would stop a competitor copying it within a year. A temporary edge is not durable advantage.`,
                    insight_delisted:       `The ESG customer de-listed Liwanag: extractive sourcing with no recovered impact cost the 15%-of-revenue account (₱7.8B) and dragged the blended return below the hurdle. The de-listing is the clearest signal the strategy reverted to the problem it was meant to solve.`,
                    insight_ownership:      `No one in the core business owned the P&L: parked in a sustainability office or the foundation, the initiative had no operating authority and no defender — exactly the structure that gets cut at the first margin squeeze.`,
                    insight_split:          `Impact and ROI were run as two budgets: the “business case” and the “impact case” never became one mechanism, which is the definition of relabeled charity rather than institutionalized social innovation.`,
                    insight_copyable:       `The differentiation rested on a copyable claim: a traceability audit or a marketing story a competitor can match next year is a marketing edge, not a barrier to imitation.`,

                    hint_philanthropy:      `You’re running a philanthropy pattern — goodwill is rising but it builds no advantage and secures no supply. Redesign so impact and supply are the same mechanism before the board reverts to the spot market.`,
                    hint_extraction:        `You’re running an extraction pattern — squeezing farmers is accelerating attrition and ESG risk. Pivot to shared value before the customer de-lists you and the supply base ages out.`,
                    hint_delisted:          `The ESG customer has de-listed you — 15% of revenue and the blended return are now capped. Recover farmer impact and rebuild the inclusive-sourcing evidence fast.`,
                    hint_hurdle:            `The blended return is below the 18% hurdle. Concentrate the envelope in the one integrated mechanism and tighten the dual-constraint discipline, or the strategy gets defunded.`,
                    hint_moat:              `Competitive advantage is thin — nothing here a rival can’t copy in a quarter. Anchor the moat in the relationships, the price floors, the platform and the proprietary data.`,
                    hint_supply:            `Coffee supply is still exposed. Lock in volume through the long-term, income-lifting mechanism rather than the spot market.`,
                    hint_strong:            `Strong position — return, impact and advantage are all healthy. Land it: lead with the mechanism, hold the strategy through the shock, and prove it with the dual-constraint metric.`,

